-- title:
--   Regularity of the cusps of Γ₁(N) for N ≥ 5
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number with $5 \le N$, and let $\pi \colon \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z}) \to \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/N)$ be the map induced on special linear groups by the reduction ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Z} \to \mathbb{Z}/N$ (`Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup.map (Int.castRingHom (ZMod N))` for $2 \times 2$ matrices). Three subgroups of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/N)$, viewed as subsets, occur: the image $\pi(\Gamma_1(N))$ of the congruence subgroup `CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma1 N`; the image $\pi(\langle T \rangle)$ of the cyclic subgroup generated by $T = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 1 \\ 0 & 1\end{pmatrix}$, i.e. of `Subgroup.zpowers ModularGroup.T`; and the image $\pi(\langle T, -1 \rangle)$ of the subgroup generated by $T$ together with the central element $-1$, i.e. of `Subgroup.zpowers ModularGroup.T ⊔ Subgroup.zpowers (-1)`. The assertion is an equality of natural-number cardinalities (`Nat.card` of `DoubleCoset.Quotient`, the set of double cosets of the ambient group $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z}/N)$ with respect to the two given subsets acting on the left and on the right): the number of double cosets $\pi(\Gamma_1(N)) \, g \, \pi(\langle T\rangle)$ equals twice the number of double cosets $\pi(\Gamma_1(N)) \, g \, \pi(\langle T, -1\rangle)$.
--
--   In classical language this is the statement that every cusp of $\Gamma_1(N)$ is regular once $N \ge 5$: the central involution $-1$ acts without fixed points on the double cosets describing the cusps, so each $\pm\langle T\rangle$-double coset splits into exactly two $\langle T\rangle$-double cosets (the hypothesis is needed, $\Gamma_1(4)$ having an irregular cusp). It feeds the cusp count used in [`CongruenceSubgroup.index_gamma1_mul_and_natCard_doubleCoset_gamma1_mul_of_prime_of_not_dvd`](thm.html#CongruenceSubgroup.index_gamma1_mul_and_natCard_doubleCoset_gamma1_mul_of_prime_of_not_dvd).
